#{[17592186357687 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357687 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357687 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357687 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357687 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cfaebf6c-bf91-568e-8656-0aaca401b3cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357687 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378018 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378018 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378018 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378018 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88349410-8bfd-560a-a802-6c2dbf91d963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378018 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & ={x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{1}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}(-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+(-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}+-2{y}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378019 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378019 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378019 #edb/r